artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider the standard families of computational models used to describe the mechanics of epithelia and multicellular tissues:

(a) Cellular Potts models, in which cells are represented by domains on a lattice and dynamics are governed by Monte Carlo updates minimizing an energy functional combining adhesion, area, and perimeter terms;
(b) Self-propelled Voronoi models, in which each cell is identified with the Voronoi region of a point in the plane and the energy of a configuration is a sum over cells of the form K_A (A_i - A_0)^2 + K_P (P_i - P_0)^2, with A_i and P_i the area and perimeter of cell i;
(c) Vertex models, in which the tissue is represented as a polygonal tiling whose vertices evolve under forces derived from a similar area/perimeter energy together with line tensions on edges.

Develop an extension of these models that simultaneously and self-consistently incorporates the following physical ingredients, and analyze the resulting rheological behavior (in particular the existence and value of characteristic time scales, critical shear rates separating flow regimes, and the size of cooperative rearrangement domains):

1. An intrinsic relaxation time scale τ and a critical shear rate γ̇_c above which the tissue response changes qualitatively;
2. A well-defined effective viscosity, addressing the difficulty that in deforming epithelia an unambiguous viscosity may not exist;
3. Spatially extended cooperative rearrangement domains rather than purely local T1 events;
4. A controlled relaxation of the two-dimensional monolayer assumption, allowing out-of-plane deformations and three-dimensional cell shapes;
5. Boundary dissipation mechanisms distinguishing cell–substrate friction from cell–cell pair dissipation;
6. Cell–substrate adhesion and the rigidity of an underlying extracellular matrix, including the case of a deformable substrate;
7. Geometries appropriate to organoids and tumor spheroids (closed surfaces, curved interfaces, finite three-dimensional aggregates);
8. Molecular-scale mechanochemical sensitivity, in which local mechanical stresses feed back on cellular parameters (line tensions, target areas/perimeters, motility) through internal biochemical variables.

Specify the equations of motion, identify the dimensionless parameters that emerge, and determine the conditions under which such an extended model exhibits a ductile-to-brittle transition in its yielding response.

